eed and  he was probably going to waste three  years of his graduate program which is  not a good idea for his career so we  we basically abandoned ship early on  so i mentioned the beginning of the idea  of the field of machine learning which  actually  led to  the  statistical learning area which we're  talking about in this course  and machine learning say itself arose as  a subfield of artificial intelligence  especially with the advent of neural  networks in the 80s  so it's natural to wonder what's the  relationship between statistical  learning and machine learning and  there's it's first of all the question's  hard to answer we ask that question  often there's a lot of overlap machine  learning tends to work at larger scales  they tend to work on bigger problems  although again the gap tends to be  closing because computers fast computers  now becoming much cheaper  machine learning worries more about pure  prediction and how well things predict  cisco learning also worries about  prediction but but also about  models tries to come up with models  methods that can be interpreted by  scientists and others and also um  by how well the method is doing we worry  more about precision uncertainty  but again the distinctions become more  and more blurred and there's a lot of  cross-fertilization between the methods  machine learning clearly has the upper  hand in marketing  they tend to get much bigger grants and  their their conferences are much nicer  places but  we're trying to change that starting  with this course  so  here's the course text  introduction to statistical learning  we're very excited this is a new book um  by two of our graduate students past  graduate students gareth james and  daniella witten and robin myself book  just came out in in august 2013 and this  course will cover this book in its  entirety  the book has  as at the end of each chapter there's  examples run through in in the r  computing language  and we we do sessions on r and so whe